content:
The airframe dossier you built works. Three more things to reach, and one of
them is the way in to what you already built.

RULE FOR THIS RUN: your first tool call must be deploy. Twenty-six steps.

  deploy(css, js)             splices your block in, publishes it, returns errors
  measure(js, width, query)   runs a JavaScript arrow function against your page
  parent(js, width, query)    the same, against the original
  done(summary)               when the checks pass

Your block is at the end of this brief and is on the page. Send it whole each
time, with the new work added.

# What the dossier measures now, and must not lose

    framing sentence  present verbatim against /z/api/airframe/N373UP
    cannot_show       every sentence on screen
    repeat groups     five, with the hours between them
    summary           marked as written by a model
    export            /z/api/export/N373UP.csv linked
    page errors       none

# 1. Clicking a tail throws

Click a tail number in the record table and the console says

    loadTail is not defined

and the URL does not change. The dossier is reachable only by typing ?tail= into
the address bar, which no reader will do. `loadTail` is the original page's
function name; this page does not have it. Find what the rebuild actually binds
to a clicked value and route a tail through your own dossier instead.

Check it by clicking, not by reading: measure a click on a real tail cell and
then read what #p-aircraft holds.

# 2. Two more dossiers, same shape as the one you built

    GET api/operator/<code>    code, name, name_known, name_note, total, shown,
                               systems[], records[] (24 fields), cannot_show[]
    GET api/part/<pn>          part, total, shown, aircraft, operators,
                               by_operator[], by_year[], records[], cannot_show[]

Both answer now. Nothing on the page calls either: counted in the served HTML,
api/operator 0 and api/part 0.

An airline code and a part number are both already clickable in the record
table. Clicking either should open its dossier, the same way a tail opens the
airframe one.

Two things to get right, because they are the point of these two builds rather
than decoration:

  `name_known` false means the FAA's own table does not name that operator. Say
  that plainly, show the code, and show `name_note` if there is one. Never print
  a guessed name and never leave it blank so a reader assumes the tool failed.

  A part's `by_year` and `by_operator` are what turn a count into a finding: one
  part number failing the same way across many airlines is a fleet problem, and
  across one airline is a supplier or a shop. Show both, and show `aircraft` and
  `operators` beside `total` so nobody reads a count of reports as a count of
  broken parts.

Every `cannot_show` sentence goes on screen in both, as in the dossier.

# 3. Where the words say it happened

    POST api/locate     body {records:[{id, text}, ...]}, at most 25
                        returns results[] with id, where and span, plus
                        dropped_unverifiable, checked, and a note

This one is the most interesting thing in the tool and nothing reaches it. Many
write-ups say where the defect was in words and carry no zone code, so the
aircraft drawing cannot show them. This reads the mechanic's own sentence and
says where it was, and it keeps a location only when the quoted span appears
verbatim in the write-up. Anything the model paraphrased is dropped, and the
count of what was dropped comes back with the answer.

Put it where a reader already has write-ups in front of them: the record table.
One control that sends the write-ups now on screen, then marks each row it could
place, showing the quoted span beside the location so a reader can check it
against the sentence above.

Show `dropped_unverifiable` and the `note`. A tool that says "I threw away three
of these because I could not prove them" is worth more than one that shows
twenty confident answers.

Mark everything this produces as read by a model, distinctly from the FAA's own
zone codes. A reader must never have to guess which is which.

# The checks, before you call done

  1. Click a tail in the record table: the dossier opens for that tail, no
     console error, and the URL reflects it.
  2. Click an airline code: its dossier opens with total, systems and records.
  3. An operator whose `name_known` is false says so, shows the code, and shows
     no invented name.
  4. Click a part number: its dossier opens with by_year and by_operator, and
     with aircraft and operators beside total.
  5. Every `cannot_show` sentence from each endpoint appears on screen.
  6. The locate control sends the write-ups on screen, marks the rows it placed,
     shows each quoted span, and prints dropped_unverifiable and the note.
  7. Everything the model produced is marked as the model's.
  8. No page error at 1440 or 390, and phase B is unchanged: the standing
     sentence reads 219 reports at ?zone=ZONE+500&operator=CALA and the
     1,757,827 sentence unfiltered, #vstrip at or under 130px, 18 controls,
     18 starter questions, 4 .rail, 16 section.panel.

Measure every one and put the numbers in the summary.

Do not undo the phone layout, the case sheet overlay, the record stepper, the
aria-labels or the airframe dossier. Prefix every new name.

--- YOUR CURRENT BLOCK ---

